 /**
@@ -13,11 +13,17 @@ Export the jwtClaims function which generates the claims for a signed user [sess
 @description
 The jwtClaims method returns a claims object to be included in a signed user [session] cookie token.
 
+The [auth]{@link module:/user/auth}, [cookie]{@link module:/user/cookie}, and [redirect]{@link module:/user/redirect} modules spread the claims into the payload after the user properties, so the claims replace any user properties with the same name. API keys signed by the [key]{@link module:/user/key} module and tokens signed by the [token]{@link module:/user/token} module do not carry these claims.
+
 The claims allow another service to authenticate its users through xyz by verifying the token type, issuer, and audience.
 
 The `typ` claim distinguishes a real user session token from a key.js API-key token.
 
-The `xyzEnv.JWT_TYPE`, `xyzEnv.JWT_ISSUER`, and `xyzEnv.JWT_AUDIENCE` variables must be configured for the claims to be included in the signed token.
+The claims are always included. Their values are read from the `xyzEnv.JWT_TYPE`, `xyzEnv.JWT_ISSUER`, and `xyzEnv.JWT_AUDIENCE` variables, which the /utils/processEnv module defaults to `session`, `xyz`, and `xyz` when they are not set. The defaults name the deployment itself; a service which verifies a different issuer or audience requires `JWT_ISSUER` and `JWT_AUDIENCE` to be set to match.
+
+The defaults are not applied to empty values. A variable which is set to an empty string, eg. declared without a value in a varlock schema, results in an empty `typ` or `iss` claim, or an `aud` claim of `['']`.
+
+The `JWT_AUDIENCE` value is split on commas into the `aud` array. Whitespace around the commas is not trimmed.
 
 @returns {object} The claims object with typ, iss, and aud properties to be merged into the signed token payload.
 */
